The data source is a timeseries of ADS-B events where each record describes the state of an aircraft at a specific time. The distinct real-world entities that can be extracted are: the aircraft itself, identified stably by the icao24 column; the observation event, identified by the combination of icao24 and time; the velocity vector (speed, bearing, vertical rate) tied to that same observation; the orientation (heading) of the aircraft at that observation; the altitude state (barometric) at that observation; the absolute geographic point (latitude/longitude) at that observation; a location wrapper that links the point as geometry; and an alternate identifier derived from the squawk code assigned to the aircraft. The aircraft column icao24 is populated for every record and uniquely identifies an airframe, making it the best subject for the Aircraft class. The observation needs both icao24 and time to be unique per record, and those columns are always present. Velocity, Orientation, Altitude, AbsolutePoint, Location, and AlternateIdentifier similarly use combinations of always-present columns (icao24, time, squawk) to build distinct IRIs. The ontology provides exact classes for Aircraft, Observation, Velocity, Orientation, Altitude, AbsolutePoint, Location, and AlternateIdentifier, and properties such as alternateName, hasAlternateIdentifier, hasObservation, observationDateTime, hasVelocity, hasOrientation, hasAltitude, hasGeographicLocation, speedMetersPerSecond, absoluteBearingAngle, inclinationAngle, headingAngle, altitudeMeters, latitudeDecimal, longitudeDecimal, hasGeometry, and alternateIdentifierValue. The callsign maps naturally to alternateName, squawk to an AlternateIdentifier value, and the numeric state columns map to the corresponding data properties by name and description. Columns onground and geoaltitude have no matching ontology term and are left unmapped. Confidence is highest for Aircraft and Observation based on the column descriptions, and slightl